6] [17592186050162 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429628 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050162 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating Tangent and Normal Lines with Parametric Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050162 #edb/ref 1033 799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050162 #edb/ref 1034 1.2723178487469127 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050162 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3308e65f-61fe-550b-8dc0-4e4a4925cb49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1033 3562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1034 1.1558263047756883 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4c6d2580-78c9-5782-9c3a-465dcd055b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1031 "Differentiating Parametric Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1033 798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1034 1.607420828424588 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050531 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "509b8ea0-3cc6-5f94-8f44-a3e6445a83e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1033 986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1034 1.0054125078076268 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050644 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5beaefa1-1d02-51a5-90bc-250adca28a57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083056 #edb/ref 1069 "e-597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083056 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083056 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083057 #edb/ref 1069 "e-598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083057 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083057 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083057 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083074 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083074 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083074 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083075 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083075 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083075 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083244 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759