17] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523015 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523022 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523025 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523026 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523029 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523064 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523073 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523079 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523083 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523089 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186523091 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083280 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Tangent and Normal Lines with Parametric Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083473 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ddf8dd4c-877a-598f-8893-a8ab19b3b308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083547 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083547 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440149 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440150 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440151 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083612 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083612 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\cos 2t + 5, y = 5\\sin 2t + 4, 0 \\le t < π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $t=0$\n- **II.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n- **III.** $t=\\pi$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083612 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57cb918d-01c6-5793-92dd-c253fd54156c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083612 #edb/ref 1074 "A vertical tangent (and horizontal normal) requires x′(t)=0 and y′(t)≠0. Here $x′(t)=- 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y′(t)=10 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. Within the given half-open interval the solutions are $\\left\\{0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}$." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186084153 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084153 #edb/ref 1061 623442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084153 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c94cfe31-d0e9-529d-b6dd-34491bfb6e2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084153 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084154 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084154 #edb/ref 1061 623443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084154 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "891625df-94b1-5558-ba83-17e23764feaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084154 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084155 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084155 #edb/ref 1061 623450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084155 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "175eff7b-218c-5515-9477-5ac8c765204a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084155 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084201 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084201 #edb/ref 1061 623447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69ed9071-e55a-5c8e-b0b2-bfbf686b3484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084203 #edb/ref 1061 623454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084203 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fadade9f-f512-5c24-92c8-f8a0cc3f8c5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084203 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084207 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084207 #edb/ref 1061 623446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084207 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a295440a-1435-58d0-bb64-ea293bda1d52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084207 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084391 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that to find the slope $m$ of a tangent line to a function $y=f(x),$ we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = f'(x)\n$$\n\nLikewise, to find the slope $m_n$ of a normal line to a function $y=f(x),$ we take the negative reciprocal of the tangent slope:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(x)}\n$$\n\nWith parametric equations $x=x(t), \\, y=y(t),$ it works the same way. The slope of the tangent line is still the derivative of $y$ with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nLikewise, the slope of the normal line is still given by the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{x'(t)}{y'(t)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084391 #edb/ref 1065 1071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084391 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48431a98-af5d-5fb6-a9c9-0c0a82d65d84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084391 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084467 #edb/ref 1064 "The slope $m$ of the tangent to a parametric curve $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to find all of the locations where the tangent to the curve is horizontal. If the tangent is horizontal, this means that $m=0,$ and so provided that $x'(t)\\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = 0\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad y'(t) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to find the locations where the tangent is horizontal, we solve the equation $y'(t) = 0.$ We also need to double-check that $x'(t)\\neq 0$ for each solution. The final step is necessary because a slope of $\\dfrac00$ is meaningless, and we cannot deduce any information regarding the tangent slope from this expression.\n\nSimilarly, if we want to find the locations where the tangent line is *vertical,* we solve the equation $x'(t)=0,$ and we also need to check that $y'(t)\\neq 0$ for each solution.\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084467 #edb/ref 1065 3964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fc76ffb8-75bd-55f8-8af4-6d84235bd0cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084467 #edb/ref 1067 "Horizontal or Vertical Tangents to Parametric Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254153 #edb/ref 1080 "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254153 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254153 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38170285-5909-5a87-b81e-7614b331e561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254545 #edb/ref 1080 "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254545 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254545 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61b95ad1-c677-5a1d-9693-7fe23f0898f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254706 #edb/ref 1080 "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254706 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254706 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74ee3251-ef46-57f3-8565-b37dbcb4bbe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255075 #edb/ref 1080 "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255075 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255075 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f808726-09d3-5692-a435-5070f152cddc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255494 #edb/ref 1080 "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255494 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255494 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186255494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256136 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7461ea8a-3c02-5867-96b2-21f3b9f03a9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256751 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256751 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256751 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256751 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256751 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e5d7b42-493e-5694-92f6-e6ea1c2f15fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256751 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272542 #edb/ref 1080 "y=x+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273199 #edb/ref 1080 "y=x+2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273199 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273199 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5e5faf98-bf15-5a2b-9fdc-09a9a727c6b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273206 #edb/ref 1080 "y=x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273206 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273206 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f37ca8d-3ea5-5dc1-85e2-3a3270cb4ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273471 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2x+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273471 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/re