egin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-6\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523065 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523089 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{5t},\\,y=4{t}^{2}-8t,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee8bc31a-d21a-5520-b1c9-d397836ee8dd" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523089 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5{e}^{5t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t-8=0\\,⟹\\,t=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=5{e}^{5}=5 e^{5}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523091 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186083280.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523091 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "dea88c52-5d24-5fd3-bddd-4f56915dffb2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186527315 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527315 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527315 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "041f8bb1-395a-5761-a87c-1b044587e237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527322 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "081ac015-24cb-5663-8688-ae1b7ff7348b" #edb/